Work-in-progress version 0.1 of esxMON memory monitor and possibly Z80
disassembler for ZX Spectrum with ESXDOS 0.8.9 supported devices. One of the
design(?) principles is to keep the code ROMmable i.e. no SMC, and no
variables outside stack or IX indexed 256 bytes "sysvar" area.

TODO:
  - transfer code to .dot-command support instead of being run from RAM
  - commandline option to load a file st startup

GENERAL:
Numbers are by default in hexdecimal. If they are preceded with _ in the
input line they are treated as decimal. 
Memory poke command also understands ASCII strings. A string is opened
with ' character and string ends to next white space of end of line.

Each command that takes <addr> as a parameter also changes the internal
memory pointer to <addr>. This implies the next 'm' or 'mm' command
without <addr> uses that previously defined <addr>.

Supported commands:
  m [<addr>]                       : show 8 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>
  mm [<addr>]                      : show 16 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>
  p <addr> [<num>|'<str>] ..       : poke bytes(!) or strings separated by
                                   : white spaces into RAM starting from
								   : <addr>
  b <bank>                         : set RAM page at $C000 to <bank>
  ls                               : show content of the current working dir
  cd <dir>                         : change current working dir
  r <file> <addr> [<off> [<len>]]  : read <file> into RAM <addr> optionally
                                   : at offset <off> (default 0) and optinally
								   : max <len> (default entire file)
  w <file> <addr> <len>            : write <file> from RAM <addr> and <len>
                                   : bytes
  f <addr1> <addr2> <byte>         : fill memory between <addr1> and <addr2>
                                   : with value <byte>
  d [<addr>]                       : Disassemble 8 opcodes starting from
                                   : <addr>
  dd [<addr>]                      : Disassemble 16 opcodes starting from
                                   : <addr>
  c <addr>                         : CALL subroutine at <addr>
  x                                : exit to BASIC
  h                                : print help page


“We should forget about small efficiencies, say about 97% of the time:
premature optimization is the root of all evil. Yet we should not pass
up our opportunities in that critical 3%.”
   Donald Knuth, “Structured Programming with Go To Statements”,
                 ACM Computing Surveys, 1974. 

